window.LUNA_CHAPTERS=(window.LUNA_CHAPTERS||[]).concat([{"number":226,"title":"Chapter 226 The Blanket","paragraphs":["\"Why are all my photos stored back here?\" Eira asked.","Gwen glanced up from the box. \"That photographer, Lainey, said she was making a documentary. She’s been asking about children who grew up here and went on to do something with their lives.\"","She gave Eira a small smile.","\"You’re the biggest success story we have. But you asked me to keep your information private, so I put everything away. I didn’t want her digging through your records or catching anything on camera.\"","A chill brushed the back of Eira’s neck.","It sounded as if Lainey had been asking about her.","Then again, a documentary covered people as well as places. Lainey’s questions were not suspicious on the surface.","\"What has she been doing since she arrived?\" Eira asked.","\"Mostly talking to people around the orphanage. She says she’s collecting stories for the film.\" Gwen frowned. \"To be honest, it feels less like a fundraising video and more like she’s looking for something.\"","\"What kind of something?\"","\"Nothing specific enough to make me stop her. If she had crossed a line, someone would’ve come to me.\"","Eira nodded, though the answer did little to ease her mind.","She turned back to the storage box and lifted a stack of old photographs. Beneath them, she found a small bundle sealed in a clear plastic bag.","Inside was a piece of blue-striped cotton.","Eira frowned. \"What is this?\"","Gwen went still.","\"That’s the blanket you were wrapped in when they found you.\"","Eira looked up. \"Mine?\"","Gwen nodded.","Every child brought to the orphanage had their clothes washed, labeled, and stored. Most of those items were ordinary. This one was not.","Eira opened the bag carefully.","The cotton was soft with age. Tiny airplanes had been stitched between the blue stripes.","\"That was expensive fabric back then,\" Gwen said quietly. \"High-end baby clothes. We thought your family might have lost you by accident instead of leaving you behind.\"","Eira ran her thumb over one of the faded airplanes.","\"We spent fifteen days checking with every neighboring Pack along the border,\" Gwen continued. \"We asked about missing pups, searched the shelters, and contacted the regional records office. Nothing came up.\"","She hesitated.","\"There was one strange detail. The blanket was made for a boy.\"","Eira’s head snapped up. \"A boy?\"","\"The pattern, the size, even the way it was sewn. Everything suggested it had been bought for a male pup.\"","Gwen lowered herself into the old office chair.","\"You know the rest. You grew up here, worked harder than anyone, and built a life far beyond this town. You never forgot the Omega House either. You’ve been giving back ever since you had the means.\"","Eira set the blanket down and wrapped her arms around Gwen.","\"Thank you,\" she whispered. \"For taking me in. For giving me a home.\"","Gwen patted her back.","\"All right. Don’t make me cry before breakfast.\"","Eira pulled away, smiling through the tightness in her throat.","Gwen’s expression softened. \"Do you want to look for your family?\"","Eira glanced at the storage box.","\"We kept the paper records. There isn’t much, but it’s a place to start.\"","Eira pressed her lips together.","\"If the answers are there, I’ll find them.\"","That was not quite an answer, and Gwen knew it.","\"If I was left on purpose, then there’s nothing to chase,\" Eira said. \"But if I was lost, my family may still be looking for me.\"","She folded the blanket back into the plastic bag.","\"I just need to give them a reason to find me.\"","Gwen reached for her hand. \"You don’t have to prove that you’re worth finding.\"","\"I know.\"","But knowing that and believing her family would find her were two different things.","People searched for what they remembered. If her family had lost a little boy, they might never think to look for the woman she had become.","Eira glanced toward the dining hall, where Lainey was still filming the children.","Lainey was the only person who had recently gone out of her way to learn about Eira’s past.","--","In Palo Alto, Alpha Killian took a screenshot of the address Eira had sent him and forwarded it to Lila without saying a word.","A voice message came back almost at once.","\"You’re so immature.\"","\"You’re the immature one!\"","Alpha Killian ignored the rest of her rant.","His flight landed a short time later. Natalie had arranged a driver, but Alpha Killian waved him off and took the wheel himself.","While he drove, he sent Eira his itinerary. She was already reviewing the information Gwen had given her about Sam, including possible routes, places to stay, and transportation options.","Eira could handle rough conditions. She had traveled alone before and knew how to take care of herself.","She was only worried about Alpha Killian.","After sending Alpha Killian the details, Eira questioned the longtime staff.","The gatekeeper, kitchen staff, and dorm supervisors gave her the same answer. Lainey had asked about the orphanage’s history and whether any former residents had gone on to become successful or appeared in the news.","Most had told her little. One of the nursery workers pulled Eira aside.","\"I mentioned you,\" she admitted. \"Miss Gwen showed me an article about your work and asked me to read it to the kids. One of them recognized you as Big Sister Eira, so I told Lainey you grew up here and now work in Palo Alto.\"","\"When?\"","\"Three days ago.\"","\"Did she ask anything else?\"","\"Not really. She thanked me and went back to playing with the children. They adore her.\"","That answer should have reassured Eira. Instead, it left her with more questions.","Eira thanked the woman and stepped back into the hallway.","\"Oh, and she was asking about the old records earlier,\" the woman added. \"I saw her heading toward the archive room.\"","Eira stopped. \"The records room?\"","\"She said she wanted some background for the documentary. I didn’t think much of it.\"","Eira thanked her and headed down the hall.","The archive door stood partly open. Inside, Lainey was filming the faded photographs and rows of filing cabinets. When she heard Eira approach, she lowered the camera and turned with a pleasant smile.","\"Eira. I was just looking for you.\"","\"Were you?\"","Lainey’s smile stayed in place, but her grip tightened around the camera.","\"I wanted footage of the older part of the building. It has character.\"","\"The records room?\"","\"I was curious.\"","\"Can I see what you filmed?\"","Lainey studied her for a moment, then handed over the camera.","\"Sure.\"","Eira skimmed the footage. It showed the grounds, the children, and a few old photographs. Nothing private or obviously invasive.","She returned the camera.","\"You’ve asked a lot about the orphanage’s history,\" Eira said. \"You also wanted to know whether any former residents became successful.\"","Lainey blinked once, then recovered.","\"History gives the documentary a stronger story. A successful former resident gives people someone to connect with. It’s good publicity.\"","\"Someone told you about me.\"","Lainey gave a small nod. \"Someone did.\"","\"If you plan to use my name or image, get permission first. The last outlet that interviewed me was a national news organization. My work involves federal security clearance, so people take the handling of my information seriously.\"","Lainey’s smile froze before smoothly returning.","\"Thanks for the warning. I wouldn’t dream of crossing a line.\"","\"Good luck with the shoot.\"","Eira turned and walked away.","The moment she disappeared around the corner, Lainey’s smile vanished. She had come to this remote stretch of Pack territory looking for something useful. Most of the staff had kept quiet, but she had finally learned that Eira had grown up there.","Then Eira had shown up and shut down the conversation.","Wasn’t she supposed to be busy dealing with Serena and the lawsuit?","Lainey pulled out her phone and called Serena.","She needed to know what had changed."]},{"number":227,"title":"Chapter 227 The Substitute","paragraphs":["Lainey slipped into a quiet corner of the courtyard and called Serena.","After a few polite words, she lowered her voice.","\"I’m out in the border territory filming. Guess who showed up?\"","Serena went quiet.","\"Eira,\" Lainey said. \"She’s back at the Omega House, nowhere near Palo Alto. That means she can’t keep an eye on you and Sebastian. You two get a little more breathing room.\"","Lainey had a talent for making every suggestion sound like a favor. She never pushed outright. She simply offered a tempting detail, then waited for someone to reach for it.","Serena took the bait.","\"Eira went back to the Omega House?\"","So that was why Alpha Sebastian had said Eira probably would not move forward with the lawsuit anytime soon.","\"Is something wrong?\" Lainey asked, her voice full of concern.","\"Something came up,\" Serena said. \"She was pressuring us hard, then suddenly backed off. She must have something more important to deal with.\"","\"Well, at least she’s off your back for now. As long as you’re okay.\"","\"Thanks, Lainey.\"","\"Of course.\" Lainey let a few seconds pass before adding, \"Still, what could be so important that she’d fly all the way out to the border?\"","Serena hesitated. \"I don’t know.\"","Lainey’s fingers tightened around her phone.","Was Eira looking for her birth family? Had someone finally uncovered a lead?","Her pulse quickened. The elevation was already making her chest feel tight.","Lainey’s body had never handled the mountain air well.She forced herself to breathe slowly.","\"Sorry,\" she said. \"I’m losing signal out here. I need to go.\"","Before Serena could ask another question, Lainey ended the call.","--","Meanwhile, Eira waited near the main entrance of the Omega House for Alpha Killian.","Her phone rang. Lila was calling.","\"Is my brother there yet?\"","\"Not yet.\" Eira glanced toward the gate. Cars passed along the highway, but none turned into the driveway.","\"So what did you bribe him with?\"","\"Eight hundred a day.\"","Lila went quiet for a second.","Then Eira heard her choke on her drink.","\"Hold on. Sorry.\" Lila coughed, then started laughing. \"Eight hundred? That’s not even a serious consulting fee for a man like my brother.\"","\"It was his number.\"","\"Please. Killian doesn’t need eight hundred dollars. He probably spends more than that on coffee.\"","Eira glanced toward the empty driveway. \"Are you done?\"","\"Not even close. With his face and that body, he could charge a fortune for a private appearance.\"","\"Lila.\"","\"What? I’m being supportive.\"","\"You’re being ridiculous.\"","\"I’m being honest. If he walked into one of those celebrity charity events, someone would pay ten grand just to have him stand next to their table.\"","Eira had no idea how to respond to that.","A car horn sounded near the gate.","She turned as a black Rolls-Royce Cullinan rolled onto the property.","Alpha Killian stepped out, tall and composed in a dark wool coat. The guard straightened before waving him through.","\"I have to go,\" Eira said.","\"What? Why?\"","\"The escort is here.\"","Lila burst out laughing.","Eira ended the call before Lila could say anything worse and hurried toward the car.","\"He’s with me,\" she told the guard.","Alpha Killian closed the driver’s door and walked toward her. The wind caught Eira’s hair and blew it across her face. She pushed it behind her ears, but the cold had already turned the tip of her nose pink.","Alpha Killian frowned and stepped in front of her, blocking the wind with his body.","\"You should have worn a hat.\"","\"I forgot.\"","\"You forgot a hat in the middle of winter?\"","\"I had other things to worry about.\" She looked toward the main building. \"Come on. Gwen wants me to bring someone I trust before I follow Sam’s lead.\"","\"Then let’s go.\"","\"You’re not tired?\"","\"No.\"","They started toward the building.","Eira lowered her voice. \"Lainey is here.\"","Alpha Killian’s expression cooled.","\"Then watch what you say.\"","\"Because of our cover?\"","\"The married-couple story.\"","Eira glanced up at him. \"Does the package include a warranty?\"","Alpha Killian looked down at her.","She had no idea where she had picked up that phrase, and he decided not to ask.","\"Yes,\" he said. \"It does.\"","For eight hundred dollars a day, Eira thought, that seemed fair.","Alpha Killian had wanted to inspect the property before they left. The Omega House sat near the edge of Pack territory, where city services were limited and patrol routes mattered. He wanted to see the security system, the fence line, and how the border Pack protected the children.","Eira was already pulling him toward Gwen’s office.","He knew Gwen’s background. She was fifty-one, had received a regional service award, and had run the Omega House for most of Eira’s life.","She was not just the director.","She was Eira’s family.","Eira knocked once and opened the door.","\"Miss Gwen, he’s here.\" She stepped aside. \"This is Killian Sterling.\"","Gwen stood and reached for her reading glasses.","\"Mr. Sterling.\"","\"Killian is fine.\" He shook her hand. \"Eira calls me that.\"","Gwen studied him for a moment. He was young, powerful, and clearly accustomed to being obeyed. Still, he had come all the way out here because Eira had asked.","That counted for something.","\"You’re prepared to protect her?\"","Alpha Killian took a business card from his wallet and set it on the desk.","\"If anything happens, call that number. If Eira is hurt because of me, the Sterling family will take responsibility.\"","His voice was quiet, but there was nothing casual about the promise.","Gwen picked up the card and looked at him more carefully.","\"Thank you,\" she said.","Alpha Killian nodded.","Gwen had seen men promise to take care of Eira before. Most of them had offered words and nothing else. Alpha Killian had come with a plan, transportation, and a way to reach him directly.","She walked them outside.","\"The county is rougher than Palo Alto,\" she warned Eira. \"Stay together, keep your phones charged, and call me if anything feels wrong.\"","\"I will.\"","They were almost at the gate when Lainey stepped out of the dining hall with her camera hanging from one shoulder.","\"Killian,\" Lainey called, bright and friendly. \"What brings you all the way out here?\"","He kept walking.","\"Not your concern.\"","Her smile tightened, but she turned to Eira instead.","\"You two seem pretty close.\"","\"Glad you can tell,\" Alpha Killian said without looking back.","A flicker of annoyance crossed Lainey’s face.","\"So where are you headed now?\"","\"Back to Palo Alto,\" Eira said.","\"Already? You just got here.\"","Before the question could turn into an interrogation, Gwen stepped in.","\"That was my doing,\" she said easily. \"I asked Eira to come back because something urgent came up. It’s taken care of now, so she needs to get going.\"","Lainey’s eyes moved from Gwen to Eira, then settled on Alpha Killian.","Urgent.","So Alpha Killian was covering for Eira. And now Gwen was covering for her too.","Lainey gave a small nod, as if she accepted the explanation.","\"Of course,\" she said. \"I hope everything worked out.\"","\"Thanks,\" Eira replied.","Lainey’s smile stayed polite, but her mind was already working.","Whatever had brought Eira here, it was more important than Lainey had expected."]},{"number":228,"title":"Chapter 228 The Search Begins","paragraphs":["The moment the SUV pulled away from the Omega House, Eira opened her tablet and pulled up the plan she had prepared earlier.","\"It’s four forty,\" she said. \"We’ll reach the county line around five thirty. We’ll grab dinner first, then check the neighborhood where Sam’s uncle used to live. Between seven and eight, people should be home and willing to talk.\"","Alpha Killian kept one hand on the wheel. His other rested near the gearshift, relaxed but ready. He glanced at her tablet, then at the focused crease between her brows.","\"After that, we can check the three high schools in the county. They all sit along the same main road, so we can cover them quickly. If we don’t find anything tonight, we start again in the morning. Then we get a hotel.\"","Eira finally looked up. \"Does that work for you?\"","\"You’re in charge.\"","\"That sounds suspiciously like you’re trying to avoid responsibility.\"","\"I’m the hired help.\" His mouth twitched. \"Eight hundred dollars a day. I follow orders.\"","The number reminded her of Lila’s ridiculous comments about Alpha Killian’s private appearance fee.","\"Killian.\"","\"Yes?\"","\"Eight hundred is not enough.\"","His silver-gray eyes slid toward her. \"Then name your price.\"","Eira hesitated. \"A thousand?\"","\"Fine.\"","He said it so easily that she narrowed her eyes. \"You didn’t even negotiate.\"","\"You were worried about underpaying me.\"","\"I was being fair.\"","\"I know.\"","The warmth in his voice made her look back at the tablet.","They reached the diner five minutes ahead of schedule. The small roadside place was bright and crowded, its windows fogged from the cold. The smell of coffee, fried onions, and warm pie filled the air.","When Alpha Killian walked in wearing a tailored coat, several heads turned. A woman at the counter whispered something to her friend, who glanced over and nearly spilled her coffee.","Eira led him to a corner booth before anyone could come over.","They ordered grilled chicken, soup, fries, and coffee. Eira chose the cheapest items on the menu, but Alpha Killian noticed.","\"Don’t worry about the bill,\" he said quietly.","\"I’m not.\"","\"You are.\"","She looked up from her menu.","\"We’re here to find Sam,\" Alpha Killian continued. \"Eat something warm. Get a decent room. Stop apologizing for taking care of yourself.\"","The words settled somewhere deep in her chest.","She closed the menu. \"Fine. But I’m still paying your fee.\"","\"I’ll send an invoice.\"","\"You’d better.\"","After dinner, they drove to the neighborhood where Sam’s uncle had lived.","The street sat beyond the county’s better-maintained roads. Puddles filled the cracked pavement, old trash cans leaned beside narrow porches, and damp smoke drifted from backyard fire pits.","A few children in puffy winter coats raced between the buildings while their parents called after them.","They had barely reached the first row of apartments when people began watching them.","A man sitting on a porch glanced at the photo on Eira’s phone and muttered, \"Wonder if they’re here about Nate.\"","Eira caught the name and approached a group of older women gathered beneath an awning.","\"Evening. Sorry to bother you. Did a man named Jordan come around here recently?\"","She showed them the photo.","One woman nodded. \"I’ve seen him. He came looking for Nate. After that, Nate told everyone the family was getting a fresh start somewhere up north.\"","\"Did he say where?\"","\"No,\" another woman said. \"He kept that part to himself.\"","\"Are you family?\" someone asked.","\"I’m Sam’s sister.\"","The women’s expressions softened.","\"You’re the girl from the Omega House,\" one of them said. \"Sam’s sister. Sweet boy.\"","\"Nate called him the family’s good-luck charm,\" another added. \"Said Sam would bring them better luck wherever they went.\"","\"Good-luck charm, my ass.\" A third woman snorted. \"That boy hauled trash, watched the little kids, and got slapped whenever they cried. Nate treated him like free labor.\"","Eira’s jaw tightened.","Then one woman lowered her voice. \"Nate used to brag about getting SSI for Sam. Said the boy brought in a few hundred dollars a month.\"","\"SSI for what?\" someone asked.","\"Autism, I think. And Nate claimed Sam was missing a thumb too.\"","Eira went still.","\"What?\"","Sam had left the Omega House with both hands intact. Eira had helped him dress. She knew exactly what she had seen.","Eira drew in a slow breath, then looked back at the women.","\"Did Nate mention where they were taking Sam?\"","The women exchanged glances.","\"Some kind of school,\" one said. \"A place for kids who need extra help.\"","\"A special school?\" another asked. \"There isn’t one in this county.\"","\"That’s still helpful,\" Eira said.","She pulled a business card from her wallet and handed it to the oldest woman.","\"If you remember anything else, please call me. Any time.\"","The woman studied the card. \"You really looking for that boy?\"","\"He’s my brother.\"","The woman’s expression softened. \"Then I hope you find him.\"","Eira thanked them and led Alpha Killian back toward the SUV.","Alpha Killian glanced at her. \"What do we know?\"","He couldn’t make out every word through the women’s overlapping voices and rapid-fire local expressions. But he saw Eira’s face change, and that told him enough.","\"Beta Jordan came looking for Nate. Nate moved the family north, claimed Sam was receiving SSI, and told people the boy was missing a thumb.\"","Alpha Killian’s jaw tightened.","\"Which isn’t true,\" Eira said. \"Sam left the Omega House with both hands intact.\"","She searched the nearby school districts on her phone.","\"The woman may have meant a special education program, not a separate school. There’s a nonpublic school in the next county, but we need to verify it.\"","\"Can we go tonight?\"","\"The schools are closed. And they can’t release Sam’s records without legal authorization.\"","\"Then I’ll make the calls.\"","While Eira checked district websites, Alpha Killian contacted his attorney. A few minutes later, Miles sent an update.","Miles: The county registrar can meet with you at eight tomorrow morning. They’ll verify whether Sam’s name appears in recent transfers, but they can’t release protected records without authorization.","Eira glanced at Alpha Killian. \"That was fast.\"","\"Money buys speed. Not access.\"","She almost smiled. \"You sound responsible.\"","\"I have my moments.\"","Eira opened the website for the specialized school. Its polished pages were full of bright photos and vague testimonials. There were no parent complaints, angry reviews, or local discussions.","That did not prove anything. Small private schools often had little online presence.","Still, the site looked too clean.","\"It’s our best lead,\" she said.","\"We’ll check it in the morning.\"","They returned to the SUV. Eira climbed into the passenger seat and kept scrolling through the school’s website.","\"Give me a minute,\" Alpha Killian said.","Before she could ask where he was going, he crossed the road to a taco truck closing beneath a string of yellow lights.","He returned with two paper bags and two cups of hot chocolate.","\"Chicken tacos,\" he said, handing her one bag. \"And churros.\"","Eira wrapped her hands around the warm cup. \"You got both?\"","\"Something spicy and something sweet.\"","The smell of grilled meat, lime, and cinnamon drifted through the cold air.","\"You went through all that trouble for tacos?\"","\"You were hungry.\"","\"I never said I was.\"","\"You didn’t have to.\"","Their fingers brushed as she took the bags.","\"Thank you.\"","After the tacos, they found a decent hotel near the highway.","The place was small but clean, with warm lighting, dark wood floors, and a front desk decorated with winter wreaths. Alpha Killian handed over his ID. Eira placed hers beside it.","\"The reservation is under my name,\" she told the clerk.","The clerk checked the screen. \"Two deluxe king rooms. Next door to each other?\"","\"That works.\"","A few minutes later, they reached the second floor.","Alpha Killian stopped outside his door. \"Get some sleep. Tomorrow is the important day.\"","\"I will.\"","She entered her room and closed the door.","A few seconds later, his door clicked shut next to hers."]}]);
